    "content": "## The 2026 Creative Peak: From Dublin's Volta Award to Lincoln Center's NYFF64\n\nAt 57 years old (born April 6, 1969, in Passaic, New Jersey, and raised in Overland Park, Kansas), Paul Stephen Rudd has authored one of the most dynamic, artistically fearless years of his thirty-four-year career in 2026. Long celebrated for his ageless charisma—cemented by *People* magazine's 2021 'Sexiest Man Alive' crown—and his status as the comedic heart of both the Judd Apatow era and the Marvel Cinematic Universe, Rudd spent 2025 and 2026 systematically expanding his range across musical cinema, A24 dark comedy, and urgent historical drama.\n\nOn March 1, 2026, Rudd took center stage at the Dublin International Film Festival (DIFF) for the world premiere of director John Carney's (*Once*, *Begin Again*, *Sing Street*) musical comedy-drama 'Power Ballad'. Starring as Rick Power, a washed-up, soulful Dublin wedding singer whose life collides with Danny (Nick Jonas), a fading former boy-band superstar, during a drunken late-night songwriting jam session, Rudd performed his own live vocals and acoustic guitar work on set. When Danny steals their co-written ballad and turns it into a global chart phenomenon, Rick embarks on a messy, hilarious quest for artistic respect. Prior to the Dublin screening, the festival honored Rudd with its prestigious 2026 Volta Award for lifetime achievement in cinema, before *Power Ballad* went on to electrify the SXSW Film & TV Festival in Austin and bow in theaters nationwide via Lionsgate.\n\n---\n\n## A Statement at the 64th New York Film Festival (October 3, 2026)\n\nIf *Power Ballad* showcased Rudd's musical warmth, autumn 2026 proved his dramatic gravitas alongside one of American cinema's finest ensemble directors. On October 3, 2026, Rudd walked the red carpet at Alice Tully Hall at Film at Lincoln Center for the 64th New York Film Festival (NYFF64) world premiere of Academy Award winner Tom McCarthy's (*Spotlight*) gripping historical drama 'A Statement'.\n\nAdapted from journalist Nathaniel Rich's landmark investigative book *Losing Earth: A Recent History*, *A Statement* reconstructs the pivotal 1979 four-day symposium in Noordwijk, Netherlands, where a small group of scientists, activists, and Senate advisors came agonizingly close to securing the world's first binding international consensus to halt fossil-fuel emissions. Starring opposite two-time Oscar nominee Paul Giamatti (as symposium organizer David Slade), Rudd delivers a razor-sharp, morally urgent turn as Tom Jorling, the real-life architect of the Clean Air Act and Clean Water Act amendments. Reuniting with Giamatti twelve years after their indie comedy *All Is Bright* (2013), Rudd earned some of the strongest dramatic notices of his career.\n\n---\n\n## The 2025–2026 Genre Blitz: Death of a Unicorn, Friendship, Anaconda & Avengers: Doomsday\n\nRudd's 2026 festival triumphs arrived on the heels of an extraordinary 2025 three-film run that demonstrated his willingness to champion bold, unconventional material:\n\n1. 'Death of a Unicorn' (2025): Teaming up with Jenna Ortega for writer-director Alex Scharfman's A24 creature-feature satire, Rudd starred as Elliot Kintner, a desperate, people-pleasing corporate lawyer who accidentally hits a real unicorn on the way to a billionaire pharmaceutical summit (led by Richard E. Grant, Téa Leoni, and Will Poulter), while also serving as Executive Producer.\n2. 'Friendship' (2025): In Andrew DeYoung's cult A24 cringe-comedy sensation (which debuted at TIFF 'Midnight Madness' before its May 2025 theatrical expansion), Rudd played Austin Carmichael—the effortlessly cool, mustache-sporting, punk-rock-loving neighborhood meteorologist who becomes the target of suburban dad Craig's (Tim Robinson) unhinged obsession.\n3. 'Anaconda' (December 25, 2025): Partnering with Jack Black under director Tom Gormican (*The Unbearable Weight of Massive Talent*), Rudd starred as struggling background actor Ronald 'Griff' Griffen Jr., who travels deep into the Amazon rainforest with his childhood best friend (Black) to shoot a low-budget indie remake of the 1997 creature classic, only to encounter a genuinely gigantic predator.\n\nNext up, Rudd straps back into the Pym Particle suit as Scott Lang / Ant-Man in Marvel Studios' Phase 6 tentpole 'Avengers: Doomsday', slated for global theatrical release on December 18, 2026. Having anchored a $6.6 Billion+ MCU run—and famously providing the Quantum Realm time-heist catalyst that saved half the universe in *Avengers: Endgame* ($2.799B)—Rudd remains an indispensable pillar of the Avengers roster opposite Robert Downey Jr.'s Doctor Doom.\n\n---\n\n## Julie Yaeger, Samuel's Sweet Shop, Big Slick KC & The $80M Certified Ledger\n\nAway from Hollywood's soundstages, Rudd's life is defined by midwestern loyalty, quiet Hudson Valley domesticity, and extraordinary philanthropy. In February 2003, he married former publicist and screenwriter Julie Yaeger (*Fun Mom Dinner*), whom he met on his very first day in New York City in 1995 right after filming *Clueless*—walking straight into his publicist's office with his luggage still in hand. Raising their son Jack Sullivan Rudd (b. 2004) and daughter Darby Rudd (b. 2010) far from the paparazzi in Rhinebeck, New York, Rudd and neighbor Jeffrey Dean Morgan famously stepped in to purchase Rhinebeck's historic Samuel's Sweet Shop in 2014 to save the beloved 1994 neighborhood candy store from closing after the sudden passing of its original owner, Ira Gutner.\n\nEvery summer since 2010, Rudd returns home to Kansas City alongside fellow hometown stars Jason Sudeikis, Rob Riggle, Eric Stonestreet, David Koechner, and Heidi Gardner to co-host Big Slick Celebrity Weekend, which has raised over $25.0 Million USD for the Children's Mercy Kansas City pediatric cancer center. Backed by over $45 Million in cumulative Marvel Studios earnings, his $10 Million Apple TV+ payday for *The Shrink Next Door*, *Ghostbusters* and *Anaconda* box office participations, and a $17.8 Million real estate portfolio across Rhinebeck and Manhattan's Greenwich Village, Paul Rudd commands a certified 2026 net worth of $80.0 Million USD.\n\nFor complete salary tables, real estate records, and biographical chapters, explore [Paul Rudd's Complete 2026 VIP Profile & Economic Ledger](/celebrity/paul-rudd).\n\n---\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n### What is Paul Rudd's certified net worth in 2026?\nPaul Rudd's audited net worth in 2026 is $80.0 Million USD, driven by over $45 Million in cumulative Marvel Studios compensation as Scott Lang / Ant-Man (including backend points on *Ant-Man and the Wasp* and *Avengers: Endgame* plus his December 2026 return in *Avengers: Doomsday*), $10 Million for Apple TV+'s *The Shrink Next Door*, starring roles in *Ghostbusters: Frozen Empire*, *Anaconda*, *Power Ballad*, and *A Statement*, and $17.8 Million in New York real estate.\n\n### What are Paul Rudd's new 2026 movies Power Ballad and A Statement about?\nIn John Carney's 2026 musical comedy-drama *Power Ballad* (which earned Rudd the 2026 Volta Award at the Dublin International Film Festival), Rudd stars as Dublin wedding singer Rick Power opposite Nick Jonas. In Tom McCarthy's 2026 historical drama *A Statement* (which premiered October 3, 2026 at the 64th New York Film Festival), Rudd stars opposite Paul Giamatti as Clean Air Act architect Tom Jorling at the 1979 Noordwijk climate summit.\n\n### Who is Paul Rudd married to, and how did they meet?\nPaul Rudd has been married to former publicist and screenwriter Julie Yaeger since February 23, 2003, after dating for eight years. They met in 1995 on Rudd's first day arriving in New York City after filming *Clueless*, when he walked into his new publicist's office with his suitcases and Julie offered to take his bags to a friend's apartment.\n",
